9,9,9],[3,3,3,6,3,3,3,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,9,9,9,9,3,9,9,8],[9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,9,9,9,9,3,9,9,8],[9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,3,9,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,6,9,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,9,9,3,6,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,7,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,9,9,9,3,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,3,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,6,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,3,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,3,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,3,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,6,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[3,3,3,6,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-fable-5-1"],"explanation":"## What the puzzle is made of\n\nEvery input has three kinds of things:\n\n1. **A legend row** near the top: a row of 2×2 blocks separated by one background cell (train 1: `2 2 8`, train 2: `4 1`, train 3: `2 2 3`). Read left to right, their colours form a repeating pattern.\n2. **One extra 2×2 block** somewhere else, in a different colour (8 in train 1, 9 in train 2, 8 in train 3). This is a *marker* colour.\n3. **Single seed cells** on the left edge (always colour = first legend colour), sitting on the boundary of a big two-colour terrain (e.g. 0/3, 8/2, 1/4).\n\n## What the output does\n\nComparing input and output:\n\n- All the 2×2 blocks (legend and marker) are **erased** – they are replaced by the colour surrounding them (the fact sheet shows `2->3`, `8->3`, `4->3` in train 1, i.e. the blocks are repainted with the ring colour).\n- From each seed a **path** is drawn. The seed is step 0; the path goes **right** through the terrain region that lies to the right of the seed, painting cells with the legend pattern cycled (`2,2,8,2,2,8,...` in train 1, `4,1,4,1,...` in train 2). When the next cell to the right is a different terrain colour (a wall), the wall cell gets the **marker colour** (4 in train 1, 9 in train 2, 8 in train 3) and the path turns **up**; when the upward step hits a wall, that wall cell is marked too and the path turns right again. The pattern index keeps counting along the whole zig-zag, not restarting at turns. The path stops when it would leave the grid, or when both continuing and turning are blocked.\n\nIn train 1 you can see this precisely: row 15 is `2 2 8 2 2 8 ... 2` then `4` at the wall (x=13), the path turns up column 12 (`2,8,2,2,8,...`), hits the 3-region at (3,12) which becomes `4`, turns right along row 4, hits (4,18) → `4`, turns up column 17, hits (0,17) → `4`, then the right turn would leave the grid, so it stops.\n\nI checked this hypothesis in code on all three train pairs – zero mismatching cells – before writing the program.\n\n## Applying it to the test inputs\n\n- **Test 1**: legend row at y=2 is `1 2 8 3`; marker block is colour 6 at (25,25); seeds are colour 1 at (11,0) and (26,0), both with region 7 to their right. Both blocks get erased to 4, then two zig-zag paths of `1,2,8,3,1,2,8,3,…` are drawn right/up through the 7 region, with 6 on every wall cell they bump into.\n- **Test 2**: legend row at y=1 is `3 3 3 6`; marker is 7 at (25,24); seeds are 3 at (14,0) and (26,0) with region 9 to their right. Same construction with pattern `3,3,3,6` and marker 7, walls being the 8 cells.\n\nConstruction stages: erase the blocks (one stage), then draw each path (one stage per seed), then emit the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    from collections import Counter\n    g = np.array(grid).copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    objs = objects(g)\n    blocks = [o for o in objs if o[\"size\"] == 4\n              and o[\"box\"][2] - o[\"box\"][0] == 1 and o[\"box\"][3] - o[\"box\"][1] == 1]\n    singles = [o for o in objs if o[\"size\"] == 1]\n\n    # legend row = the row holding most 2x2 blocks; the remaining block is the marker\n    rows = Counter(b[\"box\"][0] for b in blocks)\n    ly = rows.most_common(1)[0][0]\n    legend = sorted([b for b in blocks if b[\"box\"][0] == ly], key=lambda b: b[\"box\"][1])\n    others = [b for b in blocks if b[\"box\"][0] != ly]\n    pat = [b[\"colour\"] for b in legend]\n    mcol = others[0][\"colour\"] if others else pat[-1]\n\n    # Stage 1: erase all 2x2 blocks with the colour of their surrounding ring\n    for b in blocks:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = b[\"box\"]\n        ring = [g[y, x] for y in range(y0 - 1, y1 + 2) for x in range(x0 - 1, x1 + 2)\n                if 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W and not (y0 <= y <= y1 and x0 <= x <= x1)]\n        g[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = Counter(ring).most_common(1)[0][0]\n    emit(g.copy(), \"Erase the legend blocks and the marker block, repainting them with the surrounding colour; the legend pattern %s and marker colour %d are remembered.\" % (pat, mcol))\n\n    base = g.copy()\n    # Stage 2+: draw a right/up zig-zag path from every left-edge seed\n    for s in singles:\n        y, x = s[\"cells\"][0]\n        if x + 1 >= W:\n            continue\n        reg = base[y, x + 1]\n        dirs = [(0, 1), (-1, 0)]\n        di = 0\n        idx = 0\n        g[y, x] = pat[0]\n        while True:\n            dy, dx = dirs[di]\n            ny, nx = y + dy, x + dx\n            if not (0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W):\n                break\n            if base[ny, nx] != reg:\n                g[ny, nx] = mcol          # mark the wall cell\n                di ^= 1                   # turn (right <-> up)\n                dy, dx = dirs[di]\n                ny, nx = y + dy, x + dx\n                if not (0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W) or base[ny, nx] != reg:\n                    break\n            y, x = ny, nx\n            idx += 1\n            g[y, x] = pat[idx % len(pat)]\n        emit(g.copy(), \"From the seed at row %d on the left edge, draw the path through the %d region: go right, paint the legend pattern cyclically, put marker colour %d on each wall cell hit and alternate between right and up.\" % (s[\"cells\"][0][0], reg, mcol))\n\n    emit(g.copy(), \"All paths drawn; final grid.\")\n    return g\n","candidate_tactics":{"legend":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**A legend row** near the top: a row of 2×2 blocks separated by one background cell (train 1: `2 2 8`, train 2: `4 1`, train 3: `2 2 3`)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Single seed cells** on the left edge (always colour = first legend colour), sitting on the boundary of a big two-colour terrain (e.g. 0/3, 8/2, 1/4)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- All the 2×2 blocks (legend and marker) are **erased** – they are replaced by the colour surrounding them (the fact sheet shows `2->3`, `8->3`, `4->3` in train 1, i.e. the blocks are repainted with the ring colour)."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Read left to right, their colours form a repeating pattern."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The pattern index keeps counting along the whole zig-zag, not restarting at turns."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Both blocks get erased to 4, then two zig-zag paths of `1,2,8,3,1,2,8,3,…` are drawn right/up through the 7 region, with 6 on every wall cell they bump into."}],"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- From each seed a **path** is drawn."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The seed is step 0; the path goes **right** through the terrain region that lies to the right of the seed, painting cells with the legend pattern cycled (`2,2,8,2,2,8,...` in train 1, `4,1,4,1,...` in train 2)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":" wall), the wall cell gets the **marker colour** (4 in train 1, 9 in train 2, 8 in train 3) and the path turns **up**; when the upward step hits a wall, that wall cell is marked too and the path turns right again."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The pattern index keeps counting along the whole zig-zag, not restarting at turns."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"e8780f56c0f6d33d215d92a6c6e75d2102b05615b2c226f085c133c5734de591","task_sha256":"298ecfaec3c3941483d26814b5be7d41ee1be95aa0b502bceead8ebf888bf41f","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
